1
00:00:04,473 --> 00:00:09,495
GhostKernel: an x86-64 kernel in Rust. It boots from its own multiboot assembly into long mode.

2
00:00:09,495 --> 00:00:15,515
Five processes in ring 3, each with its own page tables, sharing the CPU through a preemptive scheduler.

3
00:00:15,515 --> 00:00:22,661
primes checkpoints itself every 3 batches. life never asked for it: the kernel snapshots it every second.

4
00:00:22,661 --> 00:00:27,689
The ghost timeline: every dot is a checkpoint. Diamonds are full images, small dots are dirty-page deltas.

5
00:00:27,689 --> 00:00:35,843
Every process is mortal unless it has a ghost: a chain of checkpoints.

6
00:00:35,843 --> 00:00:48,486
The checkpoints are real files: one FULL image, then deltas with only the pages the CPU marked dirty.

7
00:00:48,486 --> 00:00:52,853
Now break primes for real: its saved RIP becomes 0x0. The CPU raises a page fault in ring 3.

8
00:00:52,853 --> 00:00:56,691
The kernel throws away the dead address space and rebuilds it, page by page, from the checkpoint chain.

9
00:00:56,691 --> 00:01:02,385
primes wakes up inside checkpoint(), mid-computation, not at prime #0. Its log was rolled back to match: no duplicates.

10
00:01:02,385 --> 00:01:06,451
On the timeline: a red cross for the crash, a green arrow for the resurrection a few microseconds later.

11
00:01:06,451 --> 00:01:11,301
life doesn't know checkpoints exist. A general-protection fault, and...

12
00:01:11,301 --> 00:01:19,062
...the board snaps back to its last snapshot and keeps evolving.

13
00:01:19,062 --> 00:01:28,020
counter is the control group. No ghost. Same kind of fault.

14
00:01:28,020 --> 00:01:36,062
It's gone for good, along with everything it computed.

15
00:01:36,062 --> 00:01:45,500
flaky has a real bug that fires at the same step every time. Watch the kernel react.

16
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:52,918
First retry: the newest checkpoint. It crashes again, so the kernel falls back one generation...

17
00:01:52,918 --> 00:02:04,793
...and once it's clearly a crash loop, it stops instead of resurrecting it forever.

18
00:02:04,793 --> 00:02:30,055
After all of that, primes' log has every batch exactly once, in order.

19
00:02:30,055 --> 00:02:41,202
GhostKernel: processes that come back from the dead.

